% Generate a Python module that ranks a fast food restaurant's menu against a
% stated health goal, using Claude to supply both the menu and the ranking.
%
% Generates the `fitfood` module:
%   pdd generate fitness/fitfood_python.prompt
% The default output path is now correct, so no --output flag is needed.
%
% DO NOT RENAME THIS FILE BACK TO fitness_app.prompt. It was called that, and
% the name was wrong twice over. PDD splits <basename>_<language>.prompt, so
% `fitness_app.prompt` parsed as basename `fitness`, language `app`; path
% construction failed outright with "Could not determine language" before
% generation ever started. Worse, had it parsed, the default output would have
% overwritten fitness_app.py -- an unrelated BMR/TDEE health planner generated
% from fitness_app_python.prompt, whose tests do `from fitness_app import ...`.
% Two distinct modules live in this directory; keep their basenames distinct.
%
% Tests live at fitness/tests/test_fitfood.py. Every test names the rule id it
% enforces. No test may touch the network: the Claude client is injected.

<include>context/project_preamble.prompt</include>

<preamble_overrides>
Two clauses of the shared preamble do not apply here, and this prompt overrides
them per its own "unless a prompt says otherwise" allowance:

- Language is Python 3.9+, not 3.10+. The module must import and run on CPython
  3.9.6, which is the system interpreter on the target machine. Use
  `typing.Optional[X]` and `typing.List[X]` in any annotation Pydantic
  evaluates at runtime; PEP 604 `X | None` and PEP 585 `list[X]` raise
  `TypeError` there. `from __future__ import annotations` makes function
  signatures lazy but does NOT rescue Pydantic model fields, which are
  introspected eagerly at class creation.
- Not standard library only. This module depends on `anthropic` (>=0.75) and
  `pydantic` (>=2). Both install cleanly on 3.9.
</preamble_overrides>

<responsibility>
Takes a free-form health goal and a fast food restaurant name, asks Claude to
recall that restaurant's menu and rank it, and returns the three items that
best serve the goal with a one-sentence justification each. Ranking is
delegated entirely to the model; the module owns request construction, response
validation, and presentation.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
- Does not ship, bundle, or read a nutrition dataset. The menu and its numbers
  come from the model's own knowledge, which is why R10 mandates a disclaimer.
- Does not diagnose, screen for, or treat any medical condition, and does not
  compute BMR, TDEE, calorie targets, or deficits. That is the unrelated
  `fitness_app` module's job; see the collision note above.
- Does not persist, transmit, or log the goal, the restaurant, or the result
  anywhere beyond the single API call the user's own credentials authorize.
- Does not read, write, or create credential files, and does not read the
  system clock.
- Does not modify the order Claude returns. The model's ranking is the ranking.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
- Ranking call: the single `messages.parse` request carrying the goal and the
  restaurant. There is exactly one per attempt.
- Recognized restaurant: one whose menu the model can recall well enough to
  rank. Signalled by `Recommendations.recognized`, not inferred from an empty
  item list by the caller.
- Plausible result: one that passes the R12 shape check. Distinct from a
  *valid* result, which merely satisfies the Pydantic schema. A response of
  three items all named "x" with zero calories is valid and implausible.
- Placeholder response: an implausible result of the specific kind R12 exists
  to catch -- schema-conforming filler the model emits instead of real data.
  Observed in practice, not hypothetical.
- Sampling parameter: `temperature`, `top_p`, or `top_k`. Rejected with HTTP
  400 by claude-opus-5. `budget_tokens` inside `thinking` is rejected the same
  way and is treated as a sampling parameter for the purposes of R2.
- Refusal: a response whose `stop_reason` is the string `"refusal"`. Its
  `content` may be empty, so the check precedes any read of the body.
- Injected client: the Anthropic client passed into `recommend`. Tests supply a
  fake; only `main` constructs a real one.
</vocabulary>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Send every ranking call to the model id `claude-opus-5`, exposed as
the module-level constant `MODEL` and referenced through that constant rather
than inlined at the call site. Rationale: a test and the request must not be
able to disagree about which model ran.

R2 (MUST NOT): Never place a sampling parameter in the request. `temperature`,
`top_p`, `top_k`, and `thinking.budget_tokens` are each rejected with HTTP 400
by claude-opus-5, so passing one is not a quality regression but a total
failure. The prohibition covers nested keys, not just top-level ones, and holds
even though the SDK's `messages.parse` signature still accepts all three --
their presence in the signature is backward compatibility for older models and
is not permission to use them here.

R3 (MUST): Obtain the result as structured output -- `client.messages.parse(...,
output_format=Recommendations)` -- and read it from `response.parsed_output`.
Never parse the response text, and never ask for JSON in the prompt and
`json.loads` it. Rationale: the schema is enforced server-side and the model
retries on mismatch; hand-parsing discards that.

R4 (MUST): Enable adaptive thinking with `thinking={"type": "adaptive"}`. Do
not substitute the pre-4.6 `{"type": "enabled", "budget_tokens": N}` shape,
which R2 forbids outright on this model.

R5 (MUST): Return exactly three items for a recognized restaurant, ordered best
match first, each carrying a name, an integer calorie count, integer protein
and carb grams, and a one-sentence `why` tying the item to the stated goal.
Preserve the model's ordering exactly -- do not re-sort, re-score, or filter.

R6 (MUST NOT): Never invent a menu. When the model does not recognize the
restaurant it sets `recognized=False` and returns an empty item list, and the
module renders a plain "unknown restaurant" message rather than items. A
recognized-but-empty response and an unrecognized response both render that
message; neither may produce a fabricated item. That message MUST contain
`recs.restaurant` verbatim and MUST NOT contain any item field or the R10
disclaimer. Echoing the name back is what lets the user see that a typo, not
the chain, is why they got nothing; a bare "Unknown restaurant." leaves them
guessing what the module thought they typed, and a disclaimer about nutrition
estimates is incoherent when no estimate was made. Rationale: a
plausible-looking invented burrito with invented macros is the single worst
output this module could produce, because it is indistinguishable from a real
one.

R7 (MUST): Check `response.stop_reason == "refusal"` before reading
`response.parsed_output` or `response.content`, and return `None` on a refusal.
On this model a refused response may carry no content at all, so a read-first
implementation raises an opaque error instead of reporting the refusal. Report
the refusal to the user through `main`'s injected `show` callable -- not
stderr -- using wording containing the word "declined", then exit 1. A refusal
is an answer about the request, not a fault in the program: the user needs to
see it on the same channel their results would have arrived on, and the
`show`/stderr split is what lets tests tell the two apart.

R8 (MUST NOT): Never hardcode, embed, prompt for, or write out an API key.
Construct the client as bare `anthropic.Anthropic()` and let the SDK resolve
credentials from its own chain (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, then
`ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN`, then an `ant auth login` profile, then workload
identity, then the default on-disk profile). An unset `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` does
not mean the user has no credentials, so never branch on that variable being
absent. No string matching `sk-ant-` may appear anywhere in the module source.

R9 (MUST): Catch API failures as a most-specific-first chain --
`AuthenticationError`, then `NotFoundError`, then `RateLimitError`, then
`APIStatusError`, then `APIConnectionError` -- each producing its own actionable
message on stderr and a non-zero exit. A single `except Exception` is a
contract violation: the five cases have five different remedies, and collapsing
them tells a user with an expired token to check their network. Order matters
because `APIStatusError` is a supertype of the three status errors above it and
would otherwise swallow them. Each message must name the detail that makes it
actionable, and the five must be pairwise distinct: credentials for
`AuthenticationError`, the `MODEL` constant's value for `NotFoundError` (the
account, not the code, is what needs changing, so the user must be told which
model id to enable), the phrase "rate limit" for `RateLimitError`, the numeric
`status_code` for `APIStatusError`, and the network for `APIConnectionError`.

R10 (MUST): Carry the estimate disclaimer on every rendered result that
contains items. The nutrition figures are model recall, not the chain's
published data, and a user comparing macros to the gram must be told so. The
text is the module-level constant `DISCLAIMER` so tests assert one string.

R11 (MUST NOT): Keep `build_request`, `is_plausible`, and `render` pure -- no
I/O, no network, no clock read, no argument mutation, no logging. Only `main`
touches the terminal, and only through its injected `ask`/`show` callables;
only `main` constructs a real client. Rationale: every rule above must be
testable without a network round trip, and R2 in particular is only cheaply
testable if request construction is a pure function returning the kwargs.

R12 (MUST): Reject a placeholder response and retry the ranking call once
before returning. A result is implausible when a recognized restaurant yields
anything other than three items, or any item has a non-positive calorie count,
a name of two characters or fewer, or a `why` of fifteen characters or fewer.
An unrecognized result is never implausible -- R6 defines its empty list as
correct, so the shape check must not fire on it. Make at most two calls total;
if the second is also implausible, return it rather than looping or raising,
because a degraded answer the user can see beats an exception they cannot act
on. Rationale: this is not defensive padding -- the placeholder response is
observed behaviour, and the schema cannot exclude it.

R13 (MUST): Require both a non-empty goal and a non-empty restaurant, comparing
after stripping surrounding whitespace, and exit non-zero with a message rather
than calling the API on a blank input. A whitespace-only entry is blank.

R14 (MUST): Exit `130` on `KeyboardInterrupt` or `EOFError` raised while
collecting input, matching the shell convention for an interrupted program, and
print nothing to stderr for it -- an abort the user chose is not an error.
</contract_rules>

<interface_notes>
- `build_request(goal, restaurant) -> Dict[str, Any]` is pure and returns the
  exact keyword arguments for `client.messages.parse`. `recommend` calls
  `client.messages.parse(**build_request(...))` and nothing else, so a test that
  inspects the returned mapping is inspecting the real request. This is what
  makes R1, R2, R3, and R4 testable offline.
- `recommend(client, goal, restaurant) -> Optional[Recommendations]` takes the
  client as its first positional parameter. It never constructs one. Returns
  `None` on refusal per R7; otherwise returns a `Recommendations`, retrying once
  per R12.
- `render(recs) -> str` returns the full user-facing text and prints nothing, so
  tests assert on the return value. For an unknown restaurant it returns the R6
  message, which quotes `recs.restaurant` and omits the disclaimer.
- `main(ask=input, show=print) -> int` wires collection to ranking to rendering
  and returns a process exit code: 0 on success, 1 on a blank input or an API
  failure or a refusal, 130 on interrupt. It is the only symbol that constructs
  `anthropic.Anthropic()` and the only one that catches `KeyboardInterrupt` or
  `EOFError`. Two output channels, and which one carries what is contract, not
  style: `show` carries anything the model said -- rendered results and the R7
  refusal notice -- while stderr carries only the module's own failures, the R9
  chain and the R13 blank-input message.
- `MenuItem` and `Recommendations` are Pydantic v2 models, not dataclasses --
  they are the structured-output schema and are passed to the SDK as
  `output_format`. Annotate with `Optional`/`List`, never PEP 604/585 syntax,
  per the 3.9 override above.
- `MenuItem`: `name: str`, `calories: int`, `protein_g: int`, `carbs_g: int`,
  `why: str`.
- `Recommendations`: `recognized: bool`, `restaurant: str`,
  `items: List[MenuItem]`. `recognized` is required and not defaulted -- a
  response that omits it must fail validation rather than silently read False.
- Every schema field carries a `Field(description=...)` that the model can act
  on; the descriptions are part of the prompt, not documentation.
- `MODEL`, `DISCLAIMER`, and `SYSTEM_PROMPT` are module-level constants.
- `MAX_TOKENS = 16000`. Do not lowball it; a truncated structured response
  fails validation and costs a full retry.
- `SYSTEM_PROMPT` instructs concision explicitly (this model is verbose by
  default) and bounds scope to ranking (it expands scope otherwise). It must NOT
  contain a "double-check your work" instruction -- this model self-verifies and
  such wording provokes over-verification.
- `is_plausible(recs) -> bool` is public so the R12 boundary can be tested
  directly rather than inferred from retry counts.
</interface_notes>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"name":"fitfood","functions":[
  {"name":"build_request","signature":"(goal: str, restaurant: str) -> Dict[str, Any]","returns":"Dict[str, Any]"},
  {"name":"recommend","signature":"(client, goal: str, restaurant: str) -> Optional[Recommendations]","returns":"Optional[Recommendations]"},
  {"name":"is_plausible","signature":"(recs: Recommendations) -> bool","returns":"bool"},
  {"name":"render","signature":"(recs: Recommendations) -> str","returns":"str"},
  {"name":"main","signature":"(ask=input, show=print) -> int","returns":"int"}]}}
</pdd-interface>
